ARTICLE 4 — CONDITIONS AND OPEN SOURCE OBLIGATIONS
===============================================================================

4.1 Preservation of Notices. When you distribute or make network-available
    the Software or any Derivative Work, you must retain in their entirety:
    (a) all copyright notices contained in the Software;
    (b) a copy of this License;
    (c) the Notice File (`NOTICE.md`);
    (d) any other attribution, disclaimer, or proprietary notices contained in
        the Software.
    Every copy, substantial portion, distribution, package, repository,
    container image, binary, build artifact, documentation set, or product
    component containing the Software must carry these notices. Notices may
    not be removed, hidden, obscured, altered, contradicted, or made less
    visible than any of your own notices.

4.2 Attribution. When you distribute or make network-available the Software
    or any Derivative Work, you must:
    (a) include a prominent notice stating that the work is "Based on Kevrai
        Omni" or words of similar meaning;
    (b) provide a hyperlink or reference to the original Software repository
        at https://github.com/Bullobis/kevrai-omni;
    (c) indicate in a reasonable manner that you have modified the Software,
        if applicable, together with the date of modification (see also
        Section 4.9 on Modified Versions);
    (d) not remove, alter, or obscure any attribution, copyright notice, or
        license information contained in the Software.

4.3 Source Code Availability. If you distribute the Software or any Derivative
    Work in object code or executable form, you must simultaneously:
    (a) provide the complete corresponding Effective Source Code under the
        terms of this License; or
    (b) provide a written offer, valid for at least three (3) years from the
        date of distribution (and for as long as you offer support or spare
        parts for the product, whichever is longer), to provide the complete
        corresponding Effective Source Code to any third party upon request,
        at no charge beyond the reasonable cost of media and delivery.
    The corresponding source must include everything necessary to install and
    rebuild the object code, including build scripts, dependency manifests,
    configuration files, and interface definitions.

4.4 Same-License Requirement (Copyleft). Any Derivative Work that you
    distribute or make network-available must be licensed exclusively under
    the terms of this License (or, with the Licensor's prior written consent,
    under a license determined by the Licensor to be compatible with this
    License). You may not relicense the Software or any Derivative Work under
    a more permissive, proprietary, or different license, except as expressly
    authorized by a valid Commercial Authorization issued under Article 5.
    You may not enter into any agreement with any third party that contradicts
    or limits the recipients' rights under this License.

4.5 No Additional Restrictions. You may not impose any additional terms,
    conditions, or technological protection measures on the Software or any
    Derivative Work that restrict the exercise of rights granted under this
    License by any recipient, except as expressly permitted by this License.
    You may not use digital rights management (DRM), license servers, access
    keys, encryption, or any other technological measure to restrict
    recipients from exercising the rights granted under this License, except
    as required by applicable law or as expressly authorized in writing by the
    Licensor. Any contractual restriction (such as a non-disclosure
    agreement) that purports to limit a recipient's rights under this License
    is void to that extent.

4.6 Third-Party Components. The Software may incorporate or reference
    third-party components (including model weights, engines, and libraries)
    that are governed by their own upstream licenses. Your use of such
    third-party components is subject to their respective licenses. Where a
    third-party component's license is more restrictive than this License, the
    more restrictive terms apply to that component. The Licensor makes no
    representation or warranty regarding any third-party component.

    You are solely responsible for verifying that your use of any Third-Party
    Component complies with such component's upstream license. In particular,
    you must not use any Third-Party Component that is licensed for
    non-commercial use only for any Commercial Use, unless you have obtained
    a separate commercial license from the upstream rights-holder of such
    component. The NOTICE.md file identifies certain Third-Party Components
    that are subject to non-commercial restrictions; this list is for
    convenience only and is not exhaustive.

    The upstream licensors of Third-Party Components are intended
    third-party beneficiaries of this Section 4.6 and may enforce its
    obligations relating to their own components to the extent permitted by
    their licenses and applicable law.

4.7 Network Use Source Disclosure. If you make the Software or any Derivative
    Work available to users over a network (Network Use), you must make the
    complete corresponding Effective Source Code of the Software and all
    Derivative Works available to all such network users, free of charge,
    under the terms of this License. This obligation applies regardless of
    whether the Network Use is for Commercial Use or non-commercial use, and
    regardless of whether users download a copy. You may satisfy this
    obligation by:
    (a) providing a prominent, easily accessible link to the Effective Source
        Code repository in the user interface (for example, in an "About",
        "Legal", or footer section) and in the API documentation of the
        network-accessible service; or
    (b) providing the Effective Source Code upon request to any network user
        within thirty (30) days of such request, at no charge beyond the
        reasonable cost of delivery.
    The source made available must correspond to the exact version of the
    Software or Derivative Work that is being operated over the network
    (including any Modifications), and must be kept current with the running
    version.

    For the avoidance of doubt, this Section 4.7 is intended to ensure that
    the copyleft obligations of this License apply to Network Use in a manner
    analogous to Section 13 of the GNU Affero General Public License version 3
    (AGPLv3), but without incorporating the AGPLv3 itself. Containerization,
    virtualization, serverless deployment, or separation across multiple
    hosts does not reduce or avoid this obligation.

4.8 Boundary of Derivative Works; Aggregation; Arms-Length Interfaces.
    (a) The following are Derivative Works and are fully subject to the
        copyleft obligations of this License:
        (i) any file of the Software that you modify;
        (ii) any work that statically incorporates, compiles, or links the
             Software or a material portion thereof into a single executable,
             library, or address space;
        (iii) any work that dynamically links the Software and shares
             internal data structures, non-public interfaces, or a common
             address space, or that is specifically designed to depend on the
             internal implementation of the Software;
        (iv) any work that reproduces, ports, or translates a material portion
             of the Software's source code, catalog data, agent logic, or user
             interface;
        (v) any Modified Version or Fork.
    (b) The following are presumed to be separate, independent works (mere
        aggregation) and are NOT, by that fact alone, Derivative Works,
        provided they remain genuinely separate and communicate only through
        Arms-Length Interfaces:
        (i) an independent program that invokes an unmodified copy of the
             Software as a separate process through an Arms-Length Interface;
        (ii) a separate work aggregated with the Software on the same storage
             medium, container host, or distribution medium without tight
             coupling;
        (iii) a work that interoperates with the Software solely through its
             documented public APIs using generic data formats.
        This presumption is rebuttable: if a work described in this
        subsection (b) is in substance tightly integrated with, specifically
        designed around, or functionally inseparable from the Software, or is
        a Competing Product, it shall be treated as a Derivative Work
        notwithstanding the use of process separation or an Arms-Length
        Interface.
    (c) The fact that two components communicate over a network, by IPC, or
        across containers does not by itself make either a Derivative Work of
        the other; however, Network Use of the Software remains subject to
        Section 4.7 regardless of this boundary, and any arrangement whose
        purpose or effect is to avoid the copyleft obligations is addressed
        by Article 17 (Anti-Evasion).
    (d) Further examples and an explanatory decision guide are provided in
        Appendix E; in the event of conflict, this Section 4.8 prevails over
        Appendix E.

4.9 Modified Versions and Forks. When you distribute or make network-
    available a Modified Version or Fork:
    (a) it must carry prominent notices stating that you modified the
        Software and the date of any modification;
    (b) it must not be presented as, or mistaken for, the original Software;
    (c) except for the attribution required by Section 4.2, you must not use
        the names "Kevrai", "Kevrai Omni", the Software's logos, or any
        confusingly similar name or mark as the name of the Modified Version
        or Fork, and you must choose a name and visual identity that
        reasonably distinguish it from the original Software;
    (d) you must not represent the Modified Version as official, endorsed,
        certified, or approved by the Licensor;
    (e) these requirements do not apply to Modifications that you contribute
        back to and that are accepted into the official Software repository.

4.10 Modification of This License Text. You may not modify this License and
     apply the modified text to the Software. If you wish to use a modified
     version of this License for a different work that is not the Software or
     a Derivative Work, you may do so only if you rename the license, remove
     all references to "Kevrai", "Kevrai Omni", and the Licensor (except to
