ARTICLE 6 — DATA PRIVACY AND SECURITY
===============================================================================

6.1 Data Controller Responsibility. If you use the Software or its Derivative
    Works to process User Data, you are solely responsible for:
    (a) determining the legal basis for processing such User Data;
    (b) complying with all applicable data protection and privacy laws and
        regulations, including but not limited to the Personal Information
        Protection Law of the People's Republic of China (PIPL) and its
        implementing rules, the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR)
        (EU) 2016/679, the California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA), and any
        other applicable data protection laws;
    (c) providing all required notices to data subjects and obtaining any
        required consents, including any separate consent required for
        processing Sensitive Personal Data and for cross-border transfer;
    (d) implementing appropriate technical and organizational measures to
        protect User Data from unauthorized access, use, disclosure,
        alteration, or destruction;
    (e) honoring data-subject rights (including access, correction, deletion,
        portability, restriction, objection, and withdrawal of consent) within
        the timeframes required by applicable law.

6.2 No Data Collection by Licensor. The Licensor does not collect, store, or
    process any User Data through the Software. The Software is designed to
    run locally on your own infrastructure, and the Licensor has no access to
    User Data processed through the Software. You acknowledge and agree that
    the Licensor is not a data processor or data controller with respect to any
    User Data processed through the Software.

6.3 Data Localization and Cross-Border Transfer. If you transfer User Data
    across national borders, you are solely responsible for complying with all
    applicable cross-border data transfer laws and regulations, including but
    not limited to the cross-border data transfer requirements (security
    assessment, standard contract, or certification) under PIPL and the
    appropriate safeguards under GDPR. The Licensor makes no representation or
    warranty regarding the permissibility of any cross-border data transfer.

6.4 Security Incident Notification. If you become aware of or reasonably
    suspect a Security Incident affecting the Software or User Data processed
    through the Software, you must:
    (a) take immediate remedial action to contain and mitigate the Security
        Incident;
    (b) notify the Licensor in writing within seventy-two (72) hours of
        becoming aware of or reasonably suspecting the Security Incident,
        unless applicable law requires a shorter notification period, in which
        case the shorter period applies;
    (c) provide the Licensor with all information reasonably necessary to
        assess the scope and impact of the Security Incident, including its
        nature, the categories and approximate numbers of data subjects and
        records affected, likely consequences, and measures taken;
    (d) cooperate with the Licensor in any investigation or remediation
        efforts;
    (e) notify affected data subjects and regulators where required by
        applicable law, within the applicable statutory deadlines.

    This notification obligation applies regardless of whether the Security
    Incident is your fault or the fault of any third party.

6.5 Security Measures for Commercial Users. If you hold a valid Commercial
    Authorization, you must implement and maintain reasonable and appropriate
    security measures to protect the Software and User Data, including but not
    limited to:
    (a) access controls, least-privilege principles, and strong authentication
        mechanisms (including multi-factor authentication for administrative
        access);
    (b) encryption of User Data at rest and in transit using current,
        industry-standard algorithms;
    (c) regular security assessments, penetration testing, and vulnerability
        scanning, with remediation of identified vulnerabilities on a
        risk-based schedule;
    (d) secure software development practices for any Derivative Works,
        including code review, dependency management, and an up-to-date
        software bill of materials (SBOM);
    (e) logging and monitoring of access to and use of the Software, with
        tamper-resistant logs retained for at least one hundred eighty (180)
        days;
    (f) documented incident response and disaster recovery procedures, with
        periodic testing;
    (g) personnel security, confidentiality obligations, and regular security
        training;
    (h) patch management, including prompt application of security updates
        released for the Software.

6.6 Data Deletion. Upon termination of this License or any Commercial
    Authorization, or upon a data subject's valid deletion request, you must,
    as applicable, delete or anonymize all User Data processed through the
    Software in your possession or control (including backups, on their normal
    rotation cycle), and certify such deletion in writing upon the Licensor's
    request, except to the extent retention is required by applicable law.

6.7 Subprocessor Management. If you engage any third party to process User
    Data on your behalf in connection with the Software, you must ensure that
    such third party is bound by data protection obligations no less protective
    than those set forth in this Article 6 (including confidentiality and
    security obligations), conduct appropriate due diligence, and you remain
    fully liable for any acts or omissions of such third party.

6.8 Impact Assessments and High-Risk Processing. Where processing of User Data
    through the Software is likely to result in high risk to data subjects
    (including large-scale processing of Sensitive Personal Data, systematic
    monitoring of public areas, or automated decision-making with significant
    effects), you must conduct and document a data protection impact
    assessment (DPIA/PIA) before commencing such processing, and consult
    regulators where required by applicable law.

6.9 Automated Decision-Making. If you use the Software to support decisions
    that produce legal or similarly significant effects on individuals (such
    as decisions on credit, employment, insurance, housing, education, or
    access to essential services), you must ensure appropriate human
    intervention, provide individuals with the information and means required
    by applicable law to obtain human review and to contest decisions, and
    comply with all applicable rules on automated decision-making.

6.10 Children's Data. You may not knowingly process the Personal Data of
     children below the age threshold established by applicable law (for
     example, fourteen (14) under PIPL and varying ages under other regimes)
     through the Software without satisfying the heightened consent and
     protection requirements of applicable law, including verifiable parental
     consent where required.

6.11 Telemetry and Auto-Update Transparency. If any Modified Version or
     Derivative Work that you distribute or make network-available collects
     telemetry, analytics, or usage data, or includes automatic-update
     functionality, you must: (a) disclose the categories of collected data,
     their purpose, and the update mechanism in clear language before
     collection or activation; (b) obtain any consent required by applicable
     law; (c) provide a means to disable non-essential telemetry; and (d) not
     collect telemetry surreptitiously.

