APPENDIX D — SECURITY VULNERABILITY REPORTING POLICY
===============================================================================

D.1 Purpose. This Appendix D sets forth the process for reporting security
    vulnerabilities in the Software and the terms under which the Licensor
    will handle such reports. This policy is intended to encourage good-faith
    security research and to ensure that vulnerabilities are reported and
    remediated in a coordinated and responsible manner.

D.2 Reporting Channel. Security vulnerabilities should be reported to the
    Licensor by email at the address specified in Appendix A, with the subject
    line format:

        [Security Vulnerability Report] <Brief Description> — <Date>

    The report should include, at a minimum:
    (a) a detailed description of the vulnerability, including the affected
        component(s), version(s), and configuration(s);
    (b) steps to reproduce the vulnerability, including any proof-of-concept
        code or exploit;
    (c) an assessment of the potential impact and severity of the
        vulnerability;
    (d) any suggested remediation or mitigation measures;
    (e) the reporter's name and contact information (unless the reporter wishes
        to remain anonymous).

D.3 Acknowledgment and Response. The Licensor acknowledges receipt of all
    vulnerability reports within five (5) business days. The Licensor uses
    reasonable endeavors to:
    (a) validate and assess the reported vulnerability within fifteen (15)
        business days of receipt;
    (b) provide the reporter with an initial assessment of the vulnerability
        and an estimated timeline for remediation;
    (c) keep the reporter informed of the remediation progress;
    (d) acknowledge the reporter's contribution in any security advisory or
        release notes, if the reporter consents to such acknowledgment.

D.4 Coordinated Disclosure. The Licensor requests that security researchers
    allow a minimum of ninety (90) days from the date of reporting a
    vulnerability before disclosing it publicly. The Licensor may request an
    extension of the disclosure period if additional time is needed to
    remediate the vulnerability, and the Licensor will keep the researcher
    informed of the remediation progress. The Licensor may agree to a shorter
    disclosure period in writing if the vulnerability is being actively
    exploited in the wild or if there is an urgent need for public disclosure.

D.5 Safe Harbor. Good-faith security researchers who comply with this Appendix
    D and Article 10 (Security Vulnerability Disclosure) are protected from
    legal action by the Licensor as set forth in Section 10.1. The safe harbor
    applies only to acts undertaken in compliance with this Appendix D and
    Article 10.

D.6 Bug Bounty. The Licensor may, from time to time, operate a bug bounty
    program that provides rewards for valid vulnerability reports. The terms
    and conditions of any such bug bounty program shall be published in the
    Software's official repository. In the event of any conflict between a bug
    bounty program and this Appendix D, the bug bounty program terms shall
    prevail solely with respect to the specific rewards and procedures set
    forth in the bug bounty program.

D.7 No Warranty. The Licensor makes no representation or warranty regarding the
    security of the Software or the absence of security vulnerabilities. The
    Software is provided "AS IS" as set forth in Article 14 (Disclaimer of
    Warranty).

