APPENDIX E — COPYLEFT BOUNDARY GUIDE (EXPLANATORY, NON-NORMATIVE)
===============================================================================

This Appendix E explains, with examples, how Section 4.8 distinguishes a
Derivative Work (fully subject to copyleft) from a separate independent work
(mere aggregation). It is explanatory only; Section 4.8 governs in any case
of doubt, and Article 17 prevents using these examples to evade the License.

E.1 Treated as a Derivative Work (copyleft applies)
    - Editing any source file of the Software.
    - Forking the repository and distributing or hosting the Fork.
    - Statically compiling your code together with the Software into one
      binary or library.
    - Dynamically linking against the Software while sharing internal data
      structures, non-public APIs, or a common address space.
    - Porting the agent subsystem, catalog, or UI into another codebase.
    - Building a thin wrapper whose value is substantially the Software and
      that is tightly coupled to its internals.
    - Reproducing the catalog data or agent tool definitions inside another
      product.

E.2 Presumed Separate (mere aggregation) — rebuttable
    - An independent program that launches an unmodified Software process and
      talks to it only over the documented HTTP API using JSON.
    - A script that calls the Software's documented command-line interface
      with generic inputs and parses generic outputs.
    - Shipping an unrelated program alongside the Software in one installer or
      container host without tight integration.
    These remain subject to: Section 4.7 if the Software is operated over a
    network; Article 5 if the overall arrangement is Commercial Use; and
    Article 17 if the arrangement is designed to evade copyleft.

E.3 Factors that point toward "tightly coupled" (Derivative Work)
    - Sharing internal data structures or memory.
    - Depending on undocumented internals or implementation-specific behavior.
    - Replacing or re-implementing internal modules to change behavior.
    - The combined work being meaningless or materially reduced without the
      Software.
    - Marketing the combined work as an enhanced or embedded version of Kevrai
      Omni.

E.4 Factors that point toward "arms-length" (separate work)
    - Communication only through documented, stable, generic interfaces.
    - Each side can be replaced by an independent alternative implementing the
      same generic interface.
    - Separate processes, codebases, release cycles, and failure domains.
    - Generic, non-proprietary data formats.

E.5 Deployment forms
    - Containers / virtual machines: do not by themselves change the analysis;
      tightly coupled containers are still Derivative Works, and Network Use
      inside a container cluster still triggers Section 4.7.
    - Microservices: each service is assessed separately; a service that is
      itself a Derivative Work remains copylefted even behind a network
      boundary, and exposing the Software to users over that boundary triggers
      Section 4.7.
    - SaaS / cloud: always review Section 4.7 (source disclosure to network
      users) and Article 5 (Commercial Use).

